# Compare the existing Ruby and TypeScript engines on shared controlled data

Status: active. Scope: general engine completion and comparison execution.
Source: project owner direction, 2026-10-31. Related defect: issue 234.

The existing Ruby Decko implementation and the TypeScript implementations must
be compared using the same available controlled dataset and configuration.
Use the existing Ruby copy and the owned Bunny test copy for the paired
execution. A new controlled comparison may explicitly choose its starting
dataset; proving that a historical live service used that dataset is not a
prerequisite for this new experiment.

The consuming project depends on completion of the engine. Access to a newer
live-site export, a held historical import, or private live-site ancestry must
not become an engine completion dependency. Consumer-specific migration,
final-data reconciliation and public cutover remain separate downstream work.
Their existing protected-data, account, publication and resource conditions
remain in force. Choose eligible controlled test data instead of bypassing a
hold or waiting for an unrelated production export.

## Required comparison

Establish logical equality of the selected starting state on both actual
engines before comparing requests. Record source/runtime versions, data and
configuration identities, request membership and raw outcomes. Run matching
requests against Ruby and TypeScript/Bunny. Cover rendering, semantics,
persistence and captured side effects as applicable; writes use isolated owned
test state and do not send duplicate production mail or mutate real accounts.

Preserve every failure and distinguish actual engine differences from data,
configuration, unexecuted cases and test-instrument failures. A local fixture
grade, a source mapping or an old deployed bundle is not a substitute for the
requested current paired execution. Check the comparison independently before
using its result as acceptance. The existing supported behavior and backend
requirements are unchanged.

## Completion planning

Any future completion estimate must distinguish engine repair/verification
from consumer migration and final cutover. A consumer-specific data hold may
block that consumer operation, never the independent shared-data engine
comparison. The previous forecast conditioned on private historical data is
withdrawn. Replacement estimates must name the actual remaining work and the
observations supporting their scope.

Original comparison ownership reused the deployment owner for execution.
Superseding authority, Julian via Alfred on 2026-11-01: the port lead is now
the primary and sole active hosted-test executor, governed by docs/TEST-POLICY.md.
The fleet/ruby-bunny-compare worker prepares the one complete comparison and
repairs differences; distinct review remains required before acceptance.
Requests go to the single shared queue named in that policy. No duplicate
comparison lane or historical-snapshot approval exercise is required.

